    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The story begins in the late 19th century with the \"Scramble for Africa,\" where the Belgian Congo was forcibly partitioned from indigenous territories, sparking an early awakening of nationalist thought among figures like Lumumba. Following the brutal Congo Free State era (1885–1908) marked by moonshine-inducing exploitative rubber collection, the Belgian Congo League and the Mouvement National Congolais (MNC) emerged in the 1940s to demand reforms and self-governance. External pressure from the UN and persistent domestic resistance against forced labor laws kept growing, leading the MNC and its successor, MNC-PP, to mobilize mass protests and political pressure for independence from France's \"indirect governance\" model. By 1960, this decades-long struggle culminated in the clash between fervent Congolese leaders and Belgian authorities, resulting in the sudden transfer of sovereignty and the tragic end of the post-colonial era.",
